[Music]  a new children's book series first  conversations is designed to help  families talk about difficult topics  like race and gender the authors megan  madison and jessica reilly say they  wanted to write books they never had  growing up here's a reading from their  book our skin the first conversation  about race  our skin is beautiful strong and  important just the way it is  what do you love about your skin  just by looking at someone we can't tell  who they are on the inside  but sometimes people try to anyway  racism happens in lots of big and small  ways  racism can be an idea like thinking  princesses only have blonde hair  racism can be on purpose  like calling a person of color a mean  name  racism hurts  and is always unfair  that's why it's important to talk about  it  and to do our part to make things better  [Music]  people are working for racial justice  by telling the truth and sharing  feelings  by treating people the way they want to  be treated  megan madison and jessica riley thank  you so much for joining us this morning  now race and gender can be difficult for  adults  how do we break it down for kids  i think that's a really important  question and one that megan and i  thought about a lot you know as early  childhood educators  something that you have to do all the  time is take really big ideas  and break them down for very small kids  so we thought a lot about words and  concepts and sometimes our sentences  started as like 15 words and we broke  them down into maybe four or five words  and we practiced a lot on kids  we had many conversations with my own  children to kind of see what's stuck and  what they understood how old are your  kids my kids are now five and eight but  when we started writing the book they  were four and six  i wonder too megan at what age should  you start having this conversation with  kids because i remember my kids are now  34 and 35 so they're employed in potty  trained so  we're past a race conversation but when  i first started having it there were  four or five they had will in particular  they had no concept of color  like they were guessing like he said you  know you're white daddy's black they  honestly did not see color yeah yeah  every child is different  but studies show that most young  children are starting to notice  differences in skin color and also  notice big unfair patterns in the world  like why are there only boys on our  money or why in our school do the people  with white skin like why are they in  charge and all the people who cook our  food or  clean up our trash why do they have  browsing yeah kids are noticing those  patterns and developing questions  whether they have the language to  express them or not maybe they don't  know what it means exactly at what age  do you think you should start having  this conversation i mean i think it's  never too early to start so i often tell  parents right now is the right time um  but i think as soon as your children are  noticing things it's worth opening the  conversation and exploring what  questions do they have so kids are  noticing but i remember from my  childhood and i think a lot of people at  home will remember being taught color  blindness as the ideal oh you're  noticing all these things but don't  notice actually just treat everyone the  same and this book takes a very  different approach when did things shift  and why that's a great question yeah i  think that colorblind ideology that i  was also raised with does two things  first of all  it denies the lived reality of lots of  people of color in our country who are  impacted by systemic racism and who may  be treated differently because of their  skin color and then the other thing it  does is it completely shuts down the  conversation so if you can't see  something then you obviously can't talk  about it and you just can't fix anything  that you can't acknowledge and you can't  understand yeah we were all raised that  way  be colorblind and i now don't think that  that's such a good idea somebody told me  the phrase color brave and i like that  so much better than color blindness that  actually go there because talking about  race talking about now gender is very  uncomfortable for people  and i would add that you know if you  don't have these kind kinds of  conversations if you're wondering like  oh is my child seeing color i'm not sure  i'm not sure i should start the  conversation what happens is that  children do come to their own  conclusions for sure they will they will  figure it out on their own and those  conclusions often will include bias and  gender stereotypes because those are the  dominant messages in society or  influenced by somebody else that  shouldn't be leading them in these  conversations now speaking of those  conversations should it change depending  on child's race  i mean i think i'm an early childhood  educator and we use this concept of  developmentally appropriate practice  which is a very long fancy word but  essentially it means with every  conversation you want to take into  consideration both what you know about  that specific child each child is  different also what you know about in  general child development and then the  third piece is context it's going to be  a different conversation for a  two-year-old multi-racial kid growing up  in sacramento versus a six-year-old  indigenous kid growing up in oklahoma  so you're going to think about every  parent every educator is going to think  about all those different factors  because there's no one right  conversation they're all going to be a  little different no doubt about it thank  you so much for joining us megan and  madison  megan madison and jessica riley first  conversation series available wherever  you buy your books we'll be right back  after this this is awesome stuff thank  you

